NUL-terminate the advertised port string of a socket.

new_sock_info() copies the advertised port into a buffer of len + 1 with
strncpy(.., len), which writes no terminator, and never sets the last
byte - unlike the advertised address just above it. main_loop() prints
useinfo.port_no_str.s with %s into each receiver's process description,
so the description of every socket with an advertise port ends in
whatever followed in the heap. Seen under valgrind memcheck on all six
nodes at startup ("Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised
value(s)" in printf_core, origin the malloc in new_sock_info).

diff --git a/src/core/socket_info.c b/src/core/socket_info.c
index 6eacc447f5..645cdc0869 100644
--- a/src/core/socket_info.c
+++ b/src/core/socket_info.c
@@ -367,6 +367,7 @@ static inline struct socket_info *new_sock_info(char *name,
 		if(si->useinfo.port_no_str.s == NULL)
 			goto error;
 		strncpy(si->useinfo.port_no_str.s, p, si->useinfo.port_no_str.len);
+		si->useinfo.port_no_str.s[si->useinfo.port_no_str.len] = '\0';
 		si->useinfo.port_no = useport;
 
 		he = resolvehost(si->useinfo.name.s);
